# Git workflow (user-directed)

Follow this when using `git`, `gh`, or GitHub for this repository.

## Commits

- **Do not** run `git commit` automatically when finishing implementation work unless the user **explicitly** asks (e.g. “commit this”, “create a commit”).
- The user typically **commits** as part of the **`git-pr`** workflow ([.cursor/commands/git-pr.md](../commands/git-pr.md)) or another explicit step—leave changes uncommitted unless they asked for a commit.

## Pull requests

- When the user asks to **create** or **open a PR**, push the branch if needed and open a PR targeting **`master`** unless they name a different base branch.
- Use `gh` (and `GH_TOKEN` from `.env` when required: `set -a && source .env && set +a`). Never print or commit tokens.

## Merge

- **Do not merge** until the user **explicitly approves** (e.g. “merge the PR”, “I approve”, “merge it”). Do not merge on vague praise alone.
- After approval, merge on **GitHub** (`gh pr merge` or the UI). If merge fails (checks, conflicts), report and stop.

## After merge

- Sync local default branch: `git fetch origin`, `git checkout master`, `git pull origin master`.
- Remove the local feature branch when it is safe: `git branch -d <branch>` (use `-D` only if the user confirms discarding unmerged work).

## One feature at a time

- If checkout is already on a **feature** or **topic** branch with work in progress and the user asks for **another** piece of functionality, **do not** silently switch branches or start a second feature.
- **Ask** whether to: finish the current work first (PR → approval → merge → sync `master`), or pause and explicitly switch / start another branch—with their decision.
- Avoid mixing unrelated changes on one branch or branching a new feature off unfinished feature work without agreement.
